WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:21.290
Yeah.

00:00:22.310 --> 00:00:23.310
That's.

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:25.519
Thank you. That's wonderful.

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:27.619
We're taping at the blue building.

00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:30.019
And the guest of honor tonight is

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:31.999
Yascha Mounk, who is a Johns Hopkins

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:34.039
professor. Give a big hand for

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:35.040
him.

00:00:38.330 --> 00:00:40.189
And he's he's also the

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:41.899
founder of a really interesting

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:45.019
online platform magazine

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:46.639
called Persuasion.

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:48.469
And his latest book is The

00:00:48.470 --> 00:00:50.329
Identity Trap, A Story

00:00:50.330 --> 00:00:52.189
of Ideas and Power in

00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:54.109
Our Time. And I don't know if

00:00:54.110 --> 00:00:56.389
anybody out there is an academic

00:00:56.390 --> 00:00:58.099
or an intellectual.

00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:00.439
A couple people, you know,

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:02.839
mildly raising their hands.

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:04.279
One thing that's great about this

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:05.899
book is that if you believe in

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:08.029
ideas, he really

00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:09.649
demonstrates and documents that they

00:01:09.650 --> 00:01:11.479
matter, which is kind of nice to

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:12.480
hear, right.

00:01:13.670 --> 00:01:15.229
You should hope so, for sometimes

00:01:15.230 --> 00:01:16.399
perhaps it would be better if ideas

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:17.509
mattered less than they do.

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:19.699
That's right. Okay, but, Yascha

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:21.589
Mounk, thanks for coming

00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:22.999
out. And let's start.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:24.889
Give me the elevator pitch

00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:26.749
of The Identity

00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:27.439
Trap.

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:30.589
Well, the elevator pitch is that,

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:32.239
you know, there's a new set of ideas

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:34.429
about race, gender and sexual

00:01:34.430 --> 00:01:36.469
orientation that has become

00:01:36.470 --> 00:01:38.269
really influential over the last

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:39.270
decade.

00:01:40.010 --> 00:01:41.839
And in the public debate about

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:43.399
it, I think there's this weird

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:45.589
tension where

00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:47.179
on the right there's sometimes

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:49.069
attacked as

00:01:49.070 --> 00:01:50.509
destroying everything that's good

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:51.769
and holy.

00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:53.839
And sometimes people invoke terms

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:55.699
like woke or critical race theory to

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:57.109
talk about sensible things like

00:01:57.110 --> 00:01:58.249
recognizing. But there's still

00:01:58.250 --> 00:02:00.139
discrimination and sexism and racism

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:02.329
in our society, or wanting

00:02:02.330 --> 00:02:04.519
to teach kids about the history of

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:05.989
injustices in United States like

00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:07.009
slavery.

00:02:07.010 --> 00:02:08.939
And then on on, on the left and

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:10.189
the mainstream. Among many of my

00:02:10.190 --> 00:02:12.109
friends and colleagues in response

00:02:12.110 --> 00:02:13.759
to sort of suggestion that there's

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:15.589
really nothing to see here,

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:17.359
that certainly to say you're woke is

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:18.360
just to,

00:02:19.310 --> 00:02:21.529
you know, be nice and sensible

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:23.179
and want to see reality.

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:25.609
And there's really, you know,

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:28.069
the claim that the norms

00:02:28.070 --> 00:02:29.929
that organizations are run by the

00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:31.019
United States have changed is

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:32.809
just... it's just wrong.

00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:34.339
And there's nothing ideologically

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:36.199
new about what's happening

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:37.789
on the left. And so what I tried to

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:39.049
set out to do in this book is to

00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:40.999
actually understand what I think

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:42.641
is a genuinely novel ideology.

00:02:42.642 --> 00:02:43.642
An ideology may

00:02:45.710 --> 00:02:47.749
have intellectual history of its own

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:49.969
that arose in

00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:51.379
universities in the United States

00:02:51.380 --> 00:02:53.369
and other countries, and that then

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:54.289
through a series of interesting

00:02:54.290 --> 00:02:56.239
mechanisms, came to have

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:57.679
this powerful influence in our

00:02:57.680 --> 00:02:59.809
society. And I think that those

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:01.459
ideas deserve to be taken seriously

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:04.459
and then critiqued.

00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:05.389
And so that's what I do.

00:03:05.390 --> 00:03:07.219
On the first part of the book, I

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:09.559
tell the story of where these ideas

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:10.699
come from in the academy.

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:12.469
Let's start with that.

00:03:12.470 --> 00:03:14.329
You call this new

00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:16.339
set of ideas that have

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:17.779
power, that have, you know, kind of

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:19.789
taken over by your

00:03:19.790 --> 00:03:20.899
telling and I think by many

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:22.489
people's, you know, certainly the

00:03:22.490 --> 00:03:23.839
c-suites of certain types of

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:26.029
corporations, certainly the academy,

00:03:26.030 --> 00:03:27.979
increasingly different parts of

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:29.749
American life.

00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:31.459
You call that the identity

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:32.389
synthesis.

00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:34.159
Can you describe what is the

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:35.449
identity synthesis?

00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:36.529
Yeah. So first of all, we just need

00:03:36.530 --> 00:03:38.419
a damn term to refer to this

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:39.289
ideology.

00:03:39.290 --> 00:03:40.189
You know, like, and you're.

00:03:40.190 --> 00:03:42.149
You don't want to say either

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:43.339
wokeness.

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:44.389
Or political correctness, I think,

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:45.679
Oh, yeah, all of those terms.

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:47.449
But we have just very politically

00:03:47.450 --> 00:03:48.259
charged. Right?

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:49.879
So socialism.

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:51.829
Some people love socialism,

00:03:51.830 --> 00:03:53.029
but most people in this room don't

00:03:53.030 --> 00:03:54.109
like socialism.

00:03:54.110 --> 00:03:55.939
But we can all agree to call it

00:03:55.940 --> 00:03:56.899
socialism.

00:03:56.900 --> 00:03:57.900
Right.

00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:00.409
We need a term so we can discuss

00:04:00.410 --> 00:04:01.669
in a serious and neutral wave as

00:04:01.670 --> 00:04:02.539
body of ideas.

00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:03.709
And woke is not going to do it

00:04:03.710 --> 00:04:04.849
because it makes you sound like

00:04:04.850 --> 00:04:05.899
you're an old man shouting at the

00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:07.099
clouds.

00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:07.989
Okay.

00:04:07.990 --> 00:04:09.859
So I call it

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:10.819
identity synthesis.

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:12.229
Your ageist. I'm ageist.

00:04:12.230 --> 00:04:12.529
Yes.

00:04:12.530 --> 00:04:13.009
Okay.

00:04:13.010 --> 00:04:13.859
I confess.

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:15.769
That's a dangerous place to be in

00:04:15.770 --> 00:04:17.838
contemporary America.

00:04:17.839 --> 00:04:19.669
Last time I checked, you

00:04:19.670 --> 00:04:21.409
know, the don't trust anybody under

00:04:21.410 --> 00:04:22.532
80 seems to be the common thing.

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:27.980
Long list of gerontocracy.

00:04:29.330 --> 00:04:31.579
So? So I think, look, these ideas

00:04:31.580 --> 00:04:33.829
are fundamentally concerned

00:04:33.830 --> 00:04:36.229
with the role that group identities

00:04:36.230 --> 00:04:38.149
do and should play in

00:04:38.150 --> 00:04:39.529
our society.

00:04:39.530 --> 00:04:42.259
And they are a synthesis of

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:43.999
different intellectual influences,

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:45.769
in particular postmodernism,

00:04:45.770 --> 00:04:47.869
postcolonialism and critical

00:04:47.870 --> 00:04:49.129
race theory. And so that's why I

00:04:49.130 --> 00:04:51.049
call it the identity synthesis.

00:04:51.050 --> 00:04:52.336
Call it whatever you want to.

00:04:52.337 --> 00:04:53.659
Call it the thing.

00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:55.669
If we can just have a word that

00:04:55.670 --> 00:04:56.569
allows us to have a serious

00:04:56.570 --> 00:04:57.349
conversation.

00:04:57.350 --> 00:04:59.539
Let's start with those kind of

00:04:59.540 --> 00:05:00.860
three tributaries.

